From 000945a49704957991cc3ac8ef2bc02eb6229e8e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zhaosiying12138 <neucszhaopeng2016@163.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 01:58:39 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 27/42] amdgpu: drain memory traffic before retiring a wave

The implicit S_WAITCNT 0 ahead of S_ENDPGM only inspected the issued-instruction
wait counters, which can reach zero while a response is still inside a return
pipeline. Retiring released the wave's register region, so a later local-memory
completion wrote back through a zero-length region and surfaced as a bogus
"VGPR index out of range" panic several dispatches away from its cause.

Require the wave's outstanding request count to have drained as well, and add a
completion-time check in the local-memory pipeline so a completion that outlives
its wave names the responsible wave, kernel, and instruction instead of failing
later in register mapping.

Also skip decoding buffered fetch bytes for a wave that no longer holds an
execution context, and expose the existing periodic wave/pipeline progress
report through the host-dispatch config for stall diagnosis.

This is a partial repair: the same workload now advances past the former
boundary but reaches a separate LGKM accounting stall where waves wait on
s_waitcnt lgkmcnt with an empty local-memory pipeline. That imbalance is the
next investigation and is not claimed as fixed here.

